✅ 5. Demonstrate sleep()

5.1 Definition
🔴 sleep() is a static method of the Thread class.
🔴 It temporarily pauses the execution of the currently executing thread for a specified amount of time.
After the specified duration, the thread becomes eligible to run again.

Method Signatures

public static void sleep(long millis) throws InterruptedException
public static void sleep(long millis, int nanos) throws InterruptedException

5.2 Why Do We Need sleep()?

Sometimes we want to:
Delay execution.
🔴 Simulate long-running operations.
🔴 Retry an operation after some time.
🔴 Control execution speed.
🔴 Demonstrate thread scheduling.

Example:

Print 1
Wait 1 Second

Print 2
Wait 1 Second

Print 3
Without sleep() everything executes almost instantly.

5.3 Syntax
Thread.sleep(1000);

Meaning:
Pause the current thread for approximately 1000 milliseconds (1 second).

5.4 How sleep() Works Internally

Suppose:
Thread.sleep(3000);

Flow:

Current Thread
      │
      ▼
Executing
      │
      ▼
sleep(3000)
      │
      ▼
TIMED_WAITING State
      │
      ▼
3 Seconds Over
      │
      ▼
Runnable State
      │
      ▼
Scheduler Executes Again

Important:

After the sleep time expires:
🔴 The thread does not immediately start running.
🔴 It only becomes Runnable.
🔴 The scheduler decides when it actually runs.

5.5 Important Characteristics
🔴 Static method.
🔴 Belongs to Thread class.
🔴 Affects only the currently executing thread.
🔴 Throws InterruptedException.
🔴 Does not release any locks/monitors.
🔴 Used for time-based waiting.
🔴 It is non-blocking in nature.

7.6 Thread State During sleep()
Running
   │
   ▼
sleep()
   │
   ▼
TIMED_WAITING
   │
   ▼
Runnable
   │
   ▼
Running





